“Chris, lay off,” I said. The whiskey had helped reduce my headache a little bit. I picked up
Orla’s coat.
“Come on, let’s go,” I said.
“I’m fine,” she said and motioned to Chris’ half full beer.
“Let’s go,” I said again.
She slid out of the booth and I put her coat over her shoulders.
“Chris, can you get Orla home?”
“I can get home myself. I’m not a child.”
“I can do that,” Chris said. He drained his beer. She pinched her lips together and shook her
head. I glanced around. This was a common place for people to go after work and though it
